Q9 — AUTOMATE WEB APPLICATION TESTING USING SELENIUM WEBDRIVER
AIM
Create a Maven Selenium project, display a login page, automate a valid login and an invalid login, verify
the responses, capture a screenshot and inspect test results.
IMPORTANT
This is a self-contained demonstration login page for the practical. It is not a real authentication system
and must not be used to protect real accounts. The valid credentials are deliberately hard-coded for the
local demonstration.
PART A — CHECK REQUIREMENTS
1. Check Java:
java -version
Explanation: Selenium Java tests require Java to run.
2. Check the Java compiler:
javac -version
Explanation: Confirms that a JDK is installed.
3. Check Maven:
mvn -version
Explanation: Maven manages the Selenium and JUnit dependencies and runs the test.
4. Confirm Google Chrome is installed.
Explanation: The test will open Chrome and interact with the local login page.
PART B — CREATE THE MAVEN PROJECT
5. Move to the Desktop:
cd ~/Desktop
Explanation: Keeps the practical project in an easy-to-find location. In Windows Command Prompt use cd
%USERPROFILE%\Desktop.
6. Create and enter the project folder:
mkdir SeleniumLoginDemo
cd SeleniumLoginDemo
Explanation: This is the root folder for the Selenium Maven project.
7. Create the Maven folders:
mkdir -p src/main/resources
mkdir -p src/test/java
Explanation:
- src/main/resources will contain the demonstration HTML login page.
- src/test/java will contain the Selenium test class.
PART C — CREATE THE DEMONSTRATION LOGIN PAGE
8. Inside src/main/resources, create a file named login.html.
Explanation: This is the local web page Selenium will open in Chrome.
9. Enter and save this complete HTML:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Login Demo</title>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
</head>
<body>
<h2>Student Login</h2>
<label for="username">Username:</label>
<input id="username" type="text">
<br><br>
<label for="password">Password:</label>
<input id="password" type="password">
<br><br>
<button id="loginButton" onclick="login()">Login</button>
<p id="message"></p>
<script>
function login() {
var username = document.getElementById("username").value;
var password = document.getElementById("password").value;
var message = document.getElementById("message");
if (username === "admin" && password === "12345") {
message.textContent = "Login successful";
} else {
message.textContent = "Invalid username or password";
}
}
</script>
</body>
</html>
Explanation:
- The page contains username and password input fields.
- The Login button calls the JavaScript login() function.
- The demonstration accepts username admin and password 12345.
- A correct pair displays Login successful.
- Any other pair displays Invalid username or password.
- The message element has id=message so Selenium can locate and verify it.
