PART E — CREATE THE SELENIUM TEST CLASS
12. Inside src/test/java, create LoginTest.java.
Explanation: This is the automated test class Maven/JUnit will run.
13. Enter and save this Java code:
import org.junit.After;
import org.junit.Before;
import org.junit.Test;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions;
import org.openqa.selenium.OutputType;
import org.openqa.selenium.TakesScreenshot;
import org.openqa.selenium.io.FileHandler;
import java.io.File;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import static org.junit.Assert.assertEquals;
public class LoginTest {
private WebDriver driver;
@Before
public void setUp() {
ChromeOptions options = new ChromeOptions();
driver = new ChromeDriver(options);
}
@Test
public void validLoginTest() throws Exception {
String page = Paths.get("src/main/resources/login.html")
.toAbsolutePath().toUri().toString();
driver.get(page);
driver.findElement(By.id("username")).sendKeys("admin");
driver.findElement(By.id("password")).sendKeys("12345");
driver.findElement(By.id("loginButton")).click();
String message = driver.findElement(By.id("message")).getText();
assertEquals("Login successful", message);
File screenshot = ((TakesScreenshot) driver)
.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);
FileHandler.copy(screenshot, new File("valid-login.png"));
}
@Test
public void invalidLoginTest() {
String page = Paths.get("src/main/resources/login.html")
.toAbsolutePath().toUri().toString();
driver.get(page);
driver.findElement(By.id("username")).sendKeys("wronguser");
driver.findElement(By.id("password")).sendKeys("wrongpass");
driver.findElement(By.id("loginButton")).click();
String message = driver.findElement(By.id("message")).getText();
assertEquals("Invalid username or password", message);
}
@After
public void tearDown() {
if (driver != null) {
driver.quit();
}
}
}
Explanation:
- WebDriver controls the browser.
- ChromeDriver starts and controls Google Chrome.
- @Before runs setUp before each test and opens Chrome.
- driver.get opens the local HTML page.
- By.id locates page elements by their HTML id.
- sendKeys types into the username/password fields.
- click presses the Login button.
- getText reads the message displayed by the page.
- assertEquals checks that the displayed message matches the expected result.
- TakesScreenshot captures the browser screen.
- FileHandler.copy saves the screenshot as valid-login.png in the project working directory.
- @After runs after each test and closes the browser with driver.quit().
PART F — RUN SELENIUM TESTS
14. Open a terminal in the SeleniumLoginDemo root folder (the folder containing pom.xml).
Explanation: Maven needs to run from the project root.
15. Run:
mvn clean test
Explanation: Maven downloads required dependencies if needed, compiles the test and runs both
JUnit/Selenium tests.
16. Wait for Chrome to open.
Explanation: Selenium launches the browser and opens the local login page. The browser may open and
close once for each test.
17. Observe the valid login test.
Explanation: Selenium enters admin and 12345, clicks Login and checks for Login successful.
18. Observe the invalid login test.
Explanation: Selenium enters incorrect credentials, clicks Login and checks for Invalid username or
password.
19. Wait for Maven to finish and inspect the terminal summary.
Explanation: Both tests should pass if the browser opened, the page was found and the expected
messages were displayed.
20. Check the project root for valid-login.png.
Explanation: The test saves a screenshot after successful login. If tests are run from Maven, the working
directory is normally the project root.
21. Open the screenshot and verify that it shows the login page with the successful message.
Explanation: This is the screenshot evidence requested by the practical.
22. Open target/surefire-reports.
Explanation: Maven Surefire stores test execution reports here.
23. Open the report for LoginTest.
Explanation: Confirm that both validLoginTest and invalidLoginTest passed and that failures/errors are zero.
